'' AN INDIRECT CALL PASSED EVERY ARGUMENT BY VALUE, whatever the callee declared.
''
'' ParamIsByref and ParamTypeAt both read the callee's SYMBOL, and a call through a
'' pointer has none -- so a BYREF parameter got the number where it expected an address
'' and the callee then read through it, and a narrow parameter got an unconverted i64.
'' It lowered and it verified. Measured, fbc materialises a temporary per rvalue
'' ('TMP$3 = 1; (F$1)( &TMP$3, ... )') and casts for width ('(P2$0)( (int8)0 )').
''
'' The callee's SIGNATURE is what to read instead: a TK_PROC type carries its parameter
'' types, and a byref one is a TK_REFERENCE. Three goldens moved when this landed, all
'' three in the same direction -- tests/mir/lambda.out among them, which had the wrong
'' answer baked into it.
''
'' The second half is inference: '@less' had no case in InferArgType_, so a generic
'' whose parameter type IS the comparer inferred nothing and instantiated nothing.
'' generics/container-sort.bas writes eighteen of those and fb/sort.bi declares
'' SortBy that way on purpose.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/indirect-byref-args.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/indirect-byref-args.out
